/* English — the app's second language, added 30 Aug 2026.

   The keys are the Finnish source strings, because that is what SwiftUI uses as
   the key when a Text, Button, Label, navigationTitle or accessibilityLabel is
   given a string literal. Nothing in the Swift source had to change for these
   to take effect: the app was already localisable and simply had nowhere to
   look. See fi.lproj/Localizable.strings for why the Finnish table is empty.

   Finnish stays the default and the point. This exists because the app has to
   be shown to people who do not read Finnish -- the competition's judges, and
   the demo video -- and the honest way to do that is for the app to speak their
   language rather than for somebody to narrate over Finnish screens.

   To see it: -AppleLanguages "(en)" as a launch argument, or set the simulator
   to English. Nothing in the app switches language on its own.

   The register is deliberate. This app addresses an 80-year-old directly and
   plainly, never brightly, and the English says the same things the same way:
   short sentences, no exclamation, no "oops", nothing that would sound like a
   younger person being encouraging at somebody. */

